<p class="bridge">Leslie Ann Preer was 49 — a mother, with a daughter, Lauren, who lived close by. On May 2, 2001, she was found dead inside her own home on Dunmore Road in Chevy Chase, Maryland. Investigators recovered DNA from an unknown man at the scene. It matched no one in the national database, and for 23 years it stayed that way. What finally gave that DNA a name is one of the clearest examples of forensic genealogy doing exactly what it is built to do. This dossier lays it out in full.</p>

<h2>What's Inside the Dossier</h2>
<ul class="benefits">
<li><strong>The DNA that waited 23 years</strong> — the unidentified male profile from the scene, why CODIS never returned a match, and what that meant for the case</li>
<li><strong>Othram and IGG, explained</strong> — how forensic-grade genome sequencing builds a profile a standard database can't, and how a genealogical search returns <em>relatives</em>, not the suspect</li>
<li><strong>The Romanian family tree</strong> — how genealogists worked distant matches into a tree that narrowed to one family, and then to one name</li>
<li><strong>The name already in the file</strong> — how the path led to Eugene Gligor, who had dated the victim's daughter and whom investigators had spoken to back in 2001</li>
<li><strong>The legal resolution</strong> — the June 2024 arrest, the guilty plea in May 2025, and the August 2025 sentence of 22 years plus 5 years of supervised probation</li>
<li><strong>The A.I. AL analytical overlay</strong> — what the evidence establishes, what required inference, and where the line between the two sits</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why This Analysis Is Different</h2>
<div class="cred">
